Yesterday, my PIL challenging the unauthorized videos of women & minor girls on the Sacred Ghats of Ganga being uploaded on YouTube by multiple bloggers, accompanied by demeaning comments from viewers, was listed in the court of the Chief Justice of the Delhi High Court. The Court acknowledged the significance of the issue but did not issue a notice, instead granting me the liberty to pursue appropriate proceedings in accordance with the IT Rules, 2021.

The first step under the IT Rules, 2021 is to report each unauthorized video, of which there are millions, and then approach the appellate mechanism if YouTube does not block the videos. If I remain unsatisfied with the relief provided, I may approach the Court again.

I understand that everyone, including the Courts, is bound by law. My team and I will take the necessary steps as directed by the Hon'ble Court and return to the Court if the grievance is not resolved.

Additionally, a journalist from @barandbench requested a copy of the PIL. However, he did not publish a news article on the issue, citing a lack of a compelling story. This has dispelled my previous belief that @barandbench publishes news based on facts and legal matters rather than sensational stories. It is disheartening to discover that Bar & Bench prioritizes stories over reporting on genuine causes.

